# Local environment. Copy it and edit the copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored and holds real values. This file is committed, so it
# holds defaults and dummies only -- never a credential, not even a spent one.
#
# NOTHING LOADS THIS FILE ON ITS OWN. Cargo has no dotenv support and neither
# does the standard library: `app` reads the process environment, and a file
# reaches that environment only through a crate such as `dotenvy`, through
# `direnv`, through your process manager, or through
#
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#
# in the shell. This workspace deliberately takes no dotenv dependency -- see
# the README section "Environment variables: commit `.env.example`, never
# `.env`" for what to add when you want the file read for you.
#
# What follows is the documentation for every variable `app` reads. Keep it in
# step with `crates/app-cli/src/cli/config.rs`: a variable that is not listed
# here is a variable the next person has no way to discover.

# ISO 4217 currency code used when rendering money. Unset means EUR.
APP_CURRENCY=EUR

# Refuse to place an order with more lines than this. Unset means 100.
APP_MAX_ORDER_LINES=100

# --- Shape only: nothing here reads the lines below --------------------------
#
# They are kept as an illustration of the two conventions worth copying. Every
# variable is prefixed with the binary's name, so `env | grep APP_` is a
# complete answer and nothing collides with the dozens of unprefixed names
# already in a shell. And a secret is listed by name with an empty value, so a
# missing credential fails at startup with a name you can search for, rather
# than silently falling back to a default that happens to work locally.
#
# APP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/app
# APP_API_TOKEN=
